ns rather than undifferentiated corpus retrieval.", :fragment-index 15, :sentence-id "s19", :fragment {:start 1007, :end 1070, :text "embedding space and futon1a rendering; pattern-mining uses this", :intent "report", :target "shared learning-loop substrate", :rationale "The fragment states that the sibling shares the embedding and rendering substrate and consumes this mission's geometry for mining."}, :roles ["dependency" "context"], :blend-square {:schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1, :pattern "problems/llm-over-corpus-mining-lessons", :objects {:G {:text "    A section whose documentation states one shared problem carries one problem node, not per-pattern copies (the L11/L15/L18 cost finding).\n\n    + evidence: futon3 library/data-mining/README.md, opening paragraph; the L18 v3 complete search receipt runs/L18-remainder.edn.", :source {:path "futon3/library/problems/llm-over-corpus-mining-lessons.flexiarg", :sha256 "ee946ca8da244de23cb996b5e318979a7cf6f53ff83e0bb704d817e764f561e0"}, :situation-source "Single-problem-node rule and shared substrate.", :theory {:elems #{:g}, :names {:g "A shared representation permits a geometry projection without a duplicate method."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I1 {:text "    Work happens in or consumes the data-mining section's patterns.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/problems/llm-over-corpus-mining-lessons.flexiarg", :sha256 "ee946ca8da244de23cb996b5e318979a7cf6f53ff83e0bb704d817e764f561e0"}, :situation-source "futon3/library/problems/llm-over-corpus-mining-lessons.flexiarg HOWEVER/THEN.", :theory {:elems #{:i1}, :names {:i1 "Large-corpus extraction should reuse shared mining evidence."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I2 {:text "    The section's own documentation states the problem: \"Lessons distilled from the futon6 meme-mining (forward: turns -> memes/methods) and goals-and-holes (backward: turns -> C-entries/belly) runs on a rented GPU box, 2026-06-25/26. They generalize to any pipeline that runs a model over a large corpus to extract structured records\" (source: futon3 library/data-mining/README.md, opening paragraph)\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/problems/llm-over-corpus-mining-lessons.flexiarg", :sha256 "ee946ca8da244de23cb996b5e318979a7cf6f53ff83e0bb704d817e764f561e0"}, :situation-source "M-stack-geometry.md shared-substrate fragment.", :theory {:elems #{:i2}, :names {:i2 "The learning loop shares embeddings and rendering with geometry."}, :axioms #{}}}, :B {:text "    This node is the landing point for @why edges from the data-mining worklist members (mechanical pass, row L18 v3); per-pattern questions route to the section documentation, not to invention here.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/problems/llm-over-corpus-mining-lessons.flexiarg", :sha256 "ee946ca8da244de23cb996b5e318979a7cf6f53ff83e0bb704d817e764f561e0"}, :situation-source "Mission fragment and corpus-mining documentation rule.", :theory {:elems #{:b}, :names {:b "Expose geometry as a typed projection in the existing mining pipeline."}, :axioms #{}}}}, :maps {:a1 #{[:g :i1]}, :a2 #{[:g :i2]}, :b1 #{[:i1 :b]}, :b2 #{[:i2 :b]}}, :auxiliary {:b1 false, :b2 false}}} {:pattern "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph", :source-sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4", :pattern-rationale "The operator is explicitly positioned as a hypergraph-oriented analytic consumer of the geometry.", :fragment-index 17, :sentence-id "s21", :fragment {:start 1155, :end 1201, :text "M-hypergraph-operator.md` (eventual Click/Tick", :intent "report", :target "future hypergraph operator layer", :rationale "The cross-reference identifies the eventual Click/Tick operator that will consume the geometry."}, :roles ["dependency" "goal"], :blend-square {:schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1, :pattern "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph", :objects {:G {:text "    ALFWorld taught us that text descriptions of rooms and objects naturally produce first-person inhabitation language. But the futon hypergraph has the same structure: nodes are locations (files, functions, missions), hyperedges are relationships (scholia, dependencies, presence), and traversal is navigation. \"I found the multiplex function in drawbridge/core.clj\" is phenomenologically identical to \"I found the vase on the dresser.\" The difference is that the hypergraph is real and mutable — agents can add nodes, create hyperedges, annotate each other's work. The game world writes itself.\n\n    + evidence: futon4 hyperedge model (arxana-store.el:565) — {:hx/type, :hx/endpoints [{:role/source, :role/target, :role/passage}]} — N-ary typed relations stored in XTDB.\n\n    + evidence: ALFWorld session (2026-02-13) — 2 games completed with first-person language (\"I found the vase\", \"I'm at the fridge\"). Same phenomenology emerged when exploring Drawbridge code (\"P-1 and P-6 are already implemented\").\n\n    + evidence: futon3 agency.mm — Mermaid diagram showing Agency topology. Describes one subgraph of the world; the full world is the union of all futon repos as a hypergraph.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "Hypergraph-world model and operator dependency.", :theory {:elems #{:g}, :names {:g "Click/Tick analysis acts on the geometry’s real hypergraph."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I1 {:text "    You want agents to inhabit a rich, shared, mutable world where they can perceive objects, other agents, and the consequences of actions — rather than operating as isolated processes that happen to share a filesystem.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg THEN.", :theory {:elems #{:i1}, :names {:i1 "The Futon stack is the mutable shared hypergraph world."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I2 {:text "    The instinct is to build a game world as a separate layer: a dedicated server with game state, turn ordering, observation endpoints. This is how ALFWorld works (HTTP server wrapping TextWorld), and it produces a clean but isolated experience. The real work happens in the codebase, the database, the mission documents — but those feel like \"infrastructure\" rather than \"world.\" Agents treat them as APIs to query, not as places to be.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "M-stack-geometry.md operator cross-reference.", :theory {:elems #{:i2}, :names {:i2 "M-hypergraph-operator is the future Click/Tick consumer."}, :axioms #{}}}, :B {:text "    Recognize that the futon stack IS the game world. futon1 (XTDB) is the persistent map. futon2 (ant hill) is the ecology. futon3 (Agency, Drawbridge, IRC, peripherals) is the coordination layer. futon4 (Arxana) is the hypertext fabric — hyperedges with typed roles connecting documents, code, annotations, and agents. futon5 (wiring diagrams) describes the world's structure. futon6 (math dictionary / proof engine) is a quest zone. The agents are already in the game; the task is making the game legible, not building a separate one.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "Mission dependency interpreted through world-is-hypergraph.", :theory {:elems #{:b}, :names {:b "Make the real stack legible for the later operator layer."}, :axioms #{}}}}, :maps {:a1 #{[:g :i1]}, :a2 #{[:g :i2]}, :b1 #{[:i1 :b]}, :b2 #{[:i2 :b]}}, :auxiliary {:b1 false, :b2 false}}} {:pattern "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph", :source-sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4", :pattern-rationale "The hypergraph operator adds analytic behavior over the topology supplied by Stack Geometry.", :fragment-index 18, :sentence-id "s22", :fragme