claim` + `fulab-pattern-check`.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248"}, :situation-source "Evidence rule applied to the continuation.", :theory {:elems #{:g}, :names {:g "Evaluate a complete cascade by retaining the two source documents with every policy claim derived from their framings."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I1 {:text "    The agent's outputs need to be verifiable by other agents, humans, or future selves.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248"}, :situation-source "agent/evidence-over-assertion THEN/BECAUSE.", :theory {:elems #{:i1}, :names {:i1 "An interpretation should remain linked to the evidence that supports it."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I2 {:text "    Assertions without anchors are indistinguishable from confabulation. Observers cannot tell if the agent is reporting reality or generating plausible-sounding noise.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248"}, :situation-source "Framings continuation fragment.", :theory {:elems #{:i2}, :names {:i2 "The two preceding mission documents provide AIF framings."}, :axioms #{}}}, :B {:text "    Require every substantive claim to point to evidence: a file path, a trace event, a test result, a quotation. If evidence is unavailable, emit the claim as provisional with an expiry and a verification path. Make \"unsupported claim\" a detectable violation, not just a style preference.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248"}, :situation-source "Combined pattern and referenced framings.", :theory {:elems #{:b}, :names {:b "Traceable AIF framing carried into policy construction."}, :axioms #{}}}}, :maps {:a1 #{[:g :i1]}, :a2 #{[:g :i2]}, :b1 #{[:i1 :b]}, :b2 #{[:i2 :b]}}, :auxiliary {:b1 false, :b2 false}}} {:pattern "agent/evidence-over-assertion", :source-sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248", :pattern-rationale "The claim of canonical status is anchored to a specific document.", :fragment-index 17, :sentence-id "s21", :fragment {:start 1488, :end 1553, :text "- `futon5/docs/chapter0-aif-as-wiring-diagram.md` — the canonical", :intent "report", :target "canonical AIF wiring-diagram reference", :rationale "The line identifies the document treated as the canonical source for the wiring-diagram interpretation of AIF."}, :roles ["dependency" "context"], :blend-square {:schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1, :pattern "agent/evidence-over-assertion", :objects {:G {:text "    Evidence transforms agent output from \"trust me\" to \"check this.\" It enables verification, correction, and cumulative trust-building.\n\n    + evidence: Fulab PUR/PSR templates require anchors/certificates (`src/futon3/fulab/pattern_competence.clj`), and claim verification is enforced via `fulab-pattern-claim` + `fulab-pattern-check`.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248"}, :situation-source "Evidence rule applied to the canonical reference.", :theory {:elems #{:g}, :names {:g "Evaluate a complete cascade by checking its visual semantics against the named canonical exposition."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I1 {:text "    The agent's outputs need to be verifiable by other agents, humans, or future selves.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248"}, :situation-source "agent/evidence-over-assertion THEN.", :theory {:elems #{:i1}, :names {:i1 "Canonical-source claims require an exact document anchor."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I2 {:text "    Assertions without anchors are indistinguishable from confabulation. Observers cannot tell if the agent is reporting reality or generating plausible-sounding noise.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248"}, :situation-source "Canonical wiring-diagram fragment.", :theory {:elems #{:i2}, :names {:i2 "chapter0-aif-as-wiring-diagram.md is identified as the canonical exposition."}, :axioms #{}}}, :B {:text "    Require every substantive claim to point to evidence: a file path, a trace event, a test result, a quotation. If evidence is unavailable, emit the claim as provisional with an expiry and a verification path. Make \"unsupported claim\" a detectable violation, not just a style preference.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/agent/evidence-over-assertion.flexiarg", :sha256 "28425a05c4a87cbfe3147c1998e83701b8519b42c1796f1d0f25b4b039271248"}, :situation-source "Combined pattern and canonical document.", :theory {:elems #{:b}, :names {:b "A wiring representation with a verifiable canonical basis."}, :axioms #{}}}}, :maps {:a1 #{[:g :i1]}, :a2 #{[:g :i2]}, :b1 #{[:i1 :b]}, :b2 #{[:i2 :b]}}, :auxiliary {:b1 false, :b2 false}}} {:pattern "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph", :source-sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4", :pattern-rationale "A wiring diagram makes the agents, missions, and typed relationships of the real stack perceptible as a connected world.", :fragment-index 18, :sentence-id "s22", :fragment {:start 1554, :end 1622, :text "  \"AIF as wiring diagram\" exposition; the visual idiom an autonomous", :intent "report", :target "autonomous War Machine visual idiom", :rationale "The continuation identifies the canonical exposition as the source of the visual idiom for autonomous operation."}, :roles ["rationale" "dependency"], :blend-square {:schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1, :pattern "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph", :objects {:G {:text "    ALFWorld taught us that text descriptions of rooms and objects naturally produce first-person inhabitation language. But the futon hypergraph has the same structure: nodes are locations (files, functions, missions), hyperedges are relationships (scholia, dependencies, presence), and traversal is navigation. \"I found the multiplex function in drawbridge/core.clj\" is phenomenologically identical to \"I found the vase on the dresser.\" The difference is that the hypergraph is real and mutable — agents can add nodes, create hyperedges, annotate each other's work. The game world writes itself.\n\n    + evidence: futon4 hyperedge model (arxana-store.el:565) — {:hx/type, :hx/endpoints [{:role/source, :role/target, :role/passage}]} — N-ary typed relations stored in XTDB.\n\n    + evidence: ALFWorld session (2026-02-13) — 2 games completed with first-person language (\"I found the vase\", \"I'm at the fridge\"). Same phenomenology emerged when exploring Drawbridge code (\"P-1 and P-6 are already implemented\").\n\n    + evidence: futon3 agency.mm — Mermaid diagram showing Agency topology. Describes one subgraph of the world; the full world is the union of all futon repos as a hypergraph.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "Pattern legibility requirement applied to the visual idiom.", :theory {:elems #{:g}, :names {:g "Evaluate a complete cascade by whether its diagrams reveal real mission objects, roles, transitions, and consequences in the shared stack."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I1 {:text "    You want agents to inhabit a rich, shared, mutable world where they can perceive objects, other agents, and the consequences of actions — rather than operating as isolated processes that happen to share a filesystem.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph THEN/BECAUSE.", :theory {:elems #{:i1}, :names {:i1 "The stack becomes inhabitable when its actual typed relationships are visibly represented."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I2 {:text "    The instinct is to build a game world as a separate layer: a dedicated server with game state, turn ordering, observation endpoints. This is how ALFWorld works (HTTP server wrapping TextWorld), and it produces a clean but isolated experience. The real work happens in the codebase, the database, the mission documents — but those feel like \"infrastructure\" rather than \"world.\" Agents treat them as APIs to query, not as places to be.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "Visual-idiom fragment.", :theory {:elems #{:i2}, :names {:i2 "The canonical exposition supplies an autonomous War Machine visual idiom."}, :axioms #{}}}, :B {:text "    Recognize that the futon stack IS the game world. futon1 (XTDB) is the persistent map. futon2 (ant hill) is the ecology. futon3 (Agency, Drawbridge, IRC, peripherals) is the coordination layer. futon4 (Arxana) is the hypertext fabric — hyperedges with typed roles connecting documents, code, annotations, and agents. futon5 (wiring diagrams) describes the world's structure. futon6 (math dictionary / proof engine) is a quest zone. The agents are already in the game; the task is making the game legible, not building a separate one.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "Combined pattern and exposition role.", :theory {:elems #{:b}, :names {:b "A visual language that renders autonomous control as part of the actual stack world."}, :axioms #{}}}}, :maps {:a1 #{[:g :i1]}, :a2 #{[:g :i2]}, :b1 #{[:i1 :b]}, :b2 #{[:i2 :b]}}, :auxiliary {:b1 false, :b2 false}}} {:pattern "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph", :source-sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4", :pattern-rationale "Per-mission emission makes the actual mission subgraph and its relationships legible to operators and agents.", :fragment-index 19, :sentence-id "s23", :fragment {:start 1623, :end 1651, :text "  WM should emit per mission", :intent "propose", :target "per-mission War Machine diagram emission", :rationale "The continuation specifies that the autonomous War Machine should emit the visual representation for each mission."}, :roles ["action" "goal"], :blend-square {:schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1, :pattern "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph", :objects {:G {:text "    ALFWorld taught us that text descriptions of rooms and objects naturally produce first-person inhabitation language. But the futon hypergraph has the same structure: nodes are locations (files, functions, missions), hyperedges are relationships (scholia, dependencies, presence), and traversal is navigation. \"I found the multiplex function in drawbridge/core.clj\" is phenomenologically identical to \"I found the vase on the dresser.\" The difference is that the hypergraph is real and mutable — agents can add nodes, create hyperedges, annotate each other's work. The game world writes itself.\n\n    + evidence: futon4 hyperedge model (arxana-store.el:565) — {:hx/type, :hx/endpoints [{:role/source, :role/target, :role/passage}]} — N-ary typed relations stored in XTDB.\n\n    + evidence: ALFWorld session (2026-02-13) — 2 games completed with first-person language (\"I found the vase\", \"I'm at the fridge\"). Same phenomenology emerged when exploring Drawbridge code (\"P-1 and P-6 are already implemented\").\n\n    + evidence: futon3 agency.mm — Mermaid diagram showing Agency topology. Describes one subgraph of the world; the full world is the union of all futon repos as a hypergraph.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "Pattern world-legibility rule applied to per-mission emission.", :theory {:elems #{:g}, :names {:g "Evaluate a complete cascade by whether every selected mission receives a diagram of its real nodes, typed relationships, actions, and effects."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I1 {:text "    You want agents to inhabit a rich, shared, mutable world where they can perceive objects, other agents, and the consequences of actions — rather than operating as isolated processes that happen to share a filesystem.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph IF/THEN.", :theory {:elems #{:i1}, :names {:i1 "Agents need legible views of the shared mutable world they already inhabit."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I2 {:text "    The instinct is to build a game world as a separate layer: a dedicated server with game state, turn ordering, observation endpoints. This is how ALFWorld works (HTTP server wrapping TextWorld), and it produces a clean but isolated experience. The real work happens in the codebase, the database, the mission documents — but those feel like \"infrastructure\" rather than \"world.\" Agents treat them as APIs to query, not as places to be.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "Per-mission emission fragment.", :theory {:elems #{:i2}, :names {:i2 "The War Machine should emit a visual representation for every mission."}, :axioms #{}}}, :B {:text "    Recognize that the futon stack IS the game world. futon1 (XTDB) is the persistent map. futon2 (ant hill) is the ecology. futon3 (Agency, Drawbridge, IRC, peripherals) is the coordination layer. futon4 (Arxana) is the hypertext fabric — hyperedges with typed roles connecting documents, code, annotations, and agents. futon5 (wiring diagrams) describes the world's structure. futon6 (math dictionary / proof engine) is a quest zone. The agents are already in the game; the task is making the game legible, not building a separate one.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/gauntlet/world-is-hypergraph.flexiarg", :sha256 "7a16f822bbdd129e3ebf2e2810663044d0d1fa29df1dc08c48865e031fd6c6d4"}, :situation-source "Combined pattern and proposed emission.", :theory {:elems #{:b}, :names {:b "A per-mission view through which operators can inspect and navigate autonomous behavior."}, :axioms #{}}}}, :maps {:a1 #{[:g :i1]}, :a2 #{[:g :i2]}, :b1 #{[:i1 :b]}, :b2 #{[:i2 :b]}}, :auxiliary {:b1 false, :b2 false}}} {:pattern "hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph", :source-sha256 "5394a281f2def43f04a7643e1ad809a528edf3dfaaa38712902cced71475557f", :pattern-rationale "The captured mission data is source material that can be activated in the shared graph while retaining provenance.", :fragment-index 20, :sentence-id "s24", :fragment {:start 1652, :end 1718, :text "- `futon5/data/missions/futon2-aif-ants.edn` — existing capture of", :intent "report", :target "existing AIF ants mission capture", :rationale "The line identifies a durable data artifact already capturing the AIF ants mission."}, :roles ["dependency" "context"], :blend-square {:schema :wm/three-halves-square-v1, :pattern "hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph", :objects {:G {:text "      A unified, pattern-indexed graph enables reasoning/learning across boundaries while respecting each project's autonomy and\n      identity.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph.flexiarg", :sha256 "5394a281f2def43f04a7643e1ad809a528edf3dfaaa38712902cced71475557f"}, :situation-source "Activation pattern applied to the existing capture.", :theory {:elems #{:g}, :names {:g "Evaluate a complete cascade by whether it reuses the captured mission data as a provenance-linked graph source rather than reconstructing it."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I1 {:text "      Open mathematical repositories (\"deep storage\") already exist-PlanetMath, arXiv, nLab, wikis, code archives-holding\n      enormous value but lacking uniform structure and computational affordances.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph.flexiarg", :sha256 "5394a281f2def43f04a7643e1ad809a528edf3dfaaa38712902cced71475557f"}, :situation-source "hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph THEN.", :theory {:elems #{:i1}, :names {:i1 "Durable stored material should be activated in a coherent graph with provenance retained."}, :axioms #{}}}, :I2 {:text "      Treating each repository as its own universe squanders synergies and makes it hard for humans or agents to form a unified\n      mental map of mathematics.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph.flexiarg", :sha256 "5394a281f2def43f04a7643e1ad809a528edf3dfaaa38712902cced71475557f"}, :situation-source "Mission-capture fragment.", :theory {:elems #{:i2}, :names {:i2 "futon2-aif-ants.edn is an existing durable capture of the AIF ants mission."}, :axioms #{}}}, :B {:text "      Use HDM/FUTON6 as an aggregation + activation layer: ingest key fragments from deep storage into a coherent graph, annotate\n      them with patterns, proof skeletons, and cross-links, and maintain mappings back to origin sites for provenance.\n", :source {:path "futon3/library/hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph.flexiarg", :sha256 "5394a281f2def43f04a7643e1ad809a528edf3dfaaa38712902cced71475557f"}, :situation-source "Combined pattern and data artifact.", :theory {:elems #{:b}, :names {:b "Existing AIF mission data activated as a connected, source-traceable graph object."}, :axioms #{}}}}, :maps {:a1 #{[:g :i1]}, :a2 #{[:g :i2]}, :b1 #{[:i1 :b]}, :b2 #{[:i2 :b]}}, :auxiliary {:b1 false, :b2 false}}} {:pattern "hdm/deep-storage-to-active-graph", :source-sha256 "5394a281f2def43f04a7643e1ad809a528edf3dfaaa38712902cced71475557f", :pattern-rationale "The mission d